---
/**
 * HoneypotField — anti-spam decoy (Bible §17 anti-abuse). Shared by every
 * capture form so the field name lives in one place and always matches the
 * server check (`website`, see server/validation/common.ts isHoneypotTripped).
 *
 * Hidden via the `hidden` ATTRIBUTE, not a stylesheet rule: the user agent's
 * own `[hidden] { display: none }` applies even if the site stylesheet never
 * loads, so a real visitor can never see — and therefore never fill — a field
 * whose only effect would be to have their submission silently discarded.
 * That also keeps it out of the accessibility tree entirely, so it needs no
 * visible label and no axe rule applies. Bots that parse the HTML still find
 * it. Carries no `id`, so several forms can coexist on one page without
 * colliding. A filled value is never a validation error: the BFF fake-accepts
 * and stores nothing, so the bot learns nothing. Zero JS.
 */
---

<div hidden>
  <input type="text" name="website" value="" tabindex="-1" autocomplete="off" />
</div>
